course: biology
lesson: 260
title: The human body deeper: the brain and nerves
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your brain is a soft, wrinkly organ about 1.4 kilograms in an adult, made of about 86 billion nerve cells, each linked to thousands of others; together they make more connections than there are stars in our galaxy.
explain: Nerves are the body's wires: messages race along them, the fastest at about 120 metres a second, from your skin to your brain and from your brain to your muscles.
explain: Touch a hot pan and your hand jerks away before you even feel the pain: the message takes a shortcut through the spinal cord, which sends the «pull away» order straight back, and the pain reaches your brain a moment later.
explain: You use all of your brain, not just 10 percent as some films say; different areas do different jobs, like seeing, moving, speaking and remembering, and the brain is busy even while you sleep.
explain: In cartoons like Tom and Jerry, Tom gets bonked on the head, sees stars and birds spinning round, and is fine a second later; a real hard knock can shake the brain, which is called concussion, and needs rest and a doctor.
fact: A reflex is a quick action where the message goes to the spinal cord, which sends the order straight back without waiting for the brain, saving a fraction of a second.
fact: The adult brain weighs about 1.4 kilograms and has about 86 billion nerve cells, each connected to thousands of others.
fact: The brain is about 2 percent of your body weight but uses about 20 percent of your energy.
fact: Brain scans show that we use all parts of the brain, though not all at once, so the «10 percent» idea is a myth.
fact: Memories get stronger when you repeat them, sleep helps fix them as the brain replays the day, and spacing practice over several days works better than cramming.
fact: The left half of the brain controls the right side of the body and the right half controls the left, because nerves cross over in the brainstem, the stalk that joins the brain to the spinal cord.
fact: The idea that some people are «left-brained» and others «right-brained» is a myth; both halves work together.
fact: Sitting on a nerve squashes it and stops its messages for a while, and the tingle of pins and needles is the nerve waking up; moving the leg about helps it wake.
fact: The eye sees, but the brain guesses what it is seeing and can be fooled, as in optical illusions; it even fills in the blind spot where the nerve leaves each eye.
fact: You cannot tickle yourself because your brain predicts the feeling of your own fingers and makes it weaker; tickling needs surprise.
fact: The brain floats in fluid inside the hard skull, and a helmet spreads and softens a blow; after a hard knock to the head you should rest and see a doctor.
fact: Brains change as you learn, which is called plasticity, especially fast in childhood; London taxi drivers who learned all the streets were found to have a bigger memory area.
fact: You blink about 15 to 20 times a minute to wet and clean your eyes, the brain hides each dark moment, and staring at screens makes people blink less.
fact: Fat wraps fast nerves like insulation on a wire, and a stubbed toe hurts in two waves: a sharp first pain and a slower dull ache, because the two messages travel along different nerves at different speeds.
exercise: Why does your hand pull away from a hot pan before you feel the pain? | The spinal cord sends the order back without waiting for the brain | Your hand has a small brain of its own that thinks very fast for it | The pan pushes your hand away | Your eyes tell your hand to move
exercise: About how much does an adult brain weigh? | About 1.4 kilograms | About 14 kilograms | About 140 grams | About 14 grams
exercise: About how many nerve cells are in the brain? | About 86 billion | About 86 thousand | About 86 million million million | About 86
exercise: How much of the body's energy does the brain use? | About 20 percent | About 2 percent | About 90 percent | Almost none
exercise: How much of our brain do we really use? | All of it, though not all at once | Only 10 percent, the rest is fast asleep | Exactly half | Only the left side
exercise: What helps a new word stick in your memory? | Practising it and then sleeping | Hearing it only once, very carefully | Staying awake all night going over it | Never thinking about it ever again
exercise: Which half of the brain controls your right hand? | The left half | The right half | Neither, the hand controls itself | Both halves take turns each day
exercise: Is it true that some people are «left-brained» and others «right-brained»? | No, it is a myth; both halves work together | Yes, and it decides which job you will do | Yes, and you can see it clearly in brain scans | Only in left-handed people
exercise: What makes your foot «go to sleep»? | A squashed nerve that stops sending messages | The foot gets tired and has a little nap on its own | Blood turns to water in the foot | The bone in the foot bends
exercise: Why do the two lines in an illusion look different lengths? | The brain uses a shortcut that guesses wrongly | Your eyes are broken for a moment and see wrong | The lines really change size when you look away | The shiny paper bends the light on its way to you
exercise: Why can you not tickle yourself? | Your brain predicts your own touch and weakens it | Your fingers are too warm to tickle | Your skin cannot feel your own hand at all, only other people's | Only other people's skin can tickle
exercise: How does a helmet protect your brain? | It spreads and softens the blow | It makes your head lighter to carry | It keeps your brain cool and wet | It stops your brain from thinking
exercise: What does the word «plasticity» mean for the brain? | It changes and builds connections as you learn | It is made of plastic | It cannot change in any way after you are about five years old | It can be bent like rubber
exercise: What was found in London taxi drivers who learned all the streets? | A bigger memory area in their brains | Smaller brains from sitting down too long | Faster reflexes in their feet and hands | No change at all in their brains or bodies
exercise: About how often do you blink? | About 15 to 20 times a minute | About once an hour | About once a minute | About 200 times a minute
exercise: What happens to blinking when you stare at a screen? | You blink less, which dries the eyes | You blink much more, which makes your eyes wet | You stop blinking completely, for ever | It makes no difference at all to blinking
exercise: What helps the fastest nerve signals travel quickly? | A wrap of fat, like insulation on a wire | A coat of tiny hairs that sweep the messages | Blood pushing the messages along the nerves | Bones carrying them along like water pipes
test: In a cartoon, Tom is hit on the head with a frying pan, sees birds spinning round his head and jumps up a second later. What is true? | A hard knock can shake the brain, and you should rest and see a doctor | Heads bounce back from any blow at once, so you can play on straight away | Seeing birds is how the brain heals itself | Only cats get hurt by knocks on the head
test: «in that film the girl uses 100% of her brain and gets superpowers, so we only use 10% right» What is right? | No, we use all of our brain; the 10 percent idea is a myth | Yes, the other 90 percent is asleep, waiting to be woken up | Yes, but geniuses use 20 percent | No, we use only 1 percent
test: Count them: a model in the museum shows 1 brain with 2 halves and 1 spinal cord. How many halves would 3 models show? | 6 | 3 | 9 | 4
test: «gonna cram all night before the test, sleep's for losers» What would help more? | Spreading practice over days and sleeping, since sleep fixes memories | Staying awake all night, because sleep wipes out new memories | Reading the book only once | Eating sweets during the test
test: «my brother says I'm right-brained so I'm rubbish at maths» What is right? | That is a myth; both halves of the brain work together | True, right-brained people can never do maths, however hard they try | True, but it changes on Tuesdays | False, everyone is left-brained
test: What would happen if a message from a hot pan had to go all the way to the brain first? | You would pull your hand away a little later and might get a worse burn | You would never feel anything | The pan would cool down faster | You would pull away much sooner, because the brain is far quicker than the spinal cord
test: A robot tickles you a short moment after you press its button. Why might it work a bit? | The delay makes the touch less predictable for your brain | Robots have special tickly fingers made of feathers | Buttons make the skin softer | Electricity tickles on its own
test: «my leg's gone dead, pins and needles, will it fall off» What is right? | No, a squashed nerve is just waking up; moving the leg helps | Yes, if it lasts more than a minute you must call an ambulance at once | Yes, unless you keep sitting on it | No, but it will stay numb all week
test: Why might your eyes feel dry and tired after a long game on a screen? | You blink less while staring at the screen | Screens suck the water out of your eyes | Games make your eyes smaller | Blinking stops working at night
test: Your eye has a blind spot. Why do you not see a hole in the world? | The brain fills it in so you never notice | The blind spot moves around too fast for you to see it | The other ear covers it | Only old people have a blind spot
test: You stub your toe: first a sharp «ow!», then a slow dull ache. Why two waves? | The messages travel along different nerves at different speeds | The toe hurts twice because it bounces back off the door | The brain forgets the first pain | The second pain is from the other foot
exam: Identify the principal advantage conferred by a reflex action upon the withdrawal of the hand from a hot object. | The order to withdraw is issued by the spinal cord without awaiting the brain, whereby time is saved | The hand possesses an independent capacity for thought | The sensation of pain arrives prior to any movement | The eyes direct the hand without the involvement of nerves, observing the heat and commanding the muscles directly
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the proportion of the brain employed by a human being. | All regions of the brain are employed, although not all simultaneously | Merely ten percent of the brain is ever employed | Only the left hemisphere is employed by adults | The brain is wholly inactive during sleep
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the control exercised by each half of the brain over the body? | Each half governs principally the opposite side of the body, the nerves crossing in the brainstem | Each half governs the same side of the body | The left half governs the whole body during the day, the right half during the night | The halves operate in complete independence of one another, exchanging no messages
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a person is unable to tickle himself. | The brain anticipates the sensation of his own touch and diminishes it | The skin is insensible to the touch of the same body | The fingers lack the necessary warmth | Tickling is possible only during sleep
